# 5. CRITICAL VISUAL PRIORITY — HAIRSTYLE

THIS CHARACTER'S HAIRSTYLE IS ONE OF ITS MAIN IDENTIFIERS.

The owner has repeatedly emphasized that character hair must not look like a generic rectangular block.

Follow the QA concept sheet closely.

## 5.1 OVERALL SHAPE

The hair is:

- Dark chestnut brown.
- Thick and layered.
- Slightly tousled.
- Asymmetrical around the fringe.
- Voluminous around the crown.
- Gathered into a high tied-up section toward the rear/top.

The hairstyle should have a clearly readable tied-hair silhouette.

Do not model the entire hairstyle as one solid brown cube.

Do not replace it with ordinary short male hair.

Do not create extremely long free-flowing realistic hair.

Maintain the approved stylized voxel shape.

## 5.2 FRONT FRINGE

Use:

- Layered angular hair clusters.
- A recognizable side-swept fringe.
- Subtle stepped edges.
- A natural forehead opening.
- Controlled unevenness.

The fringe should frame the face without covering the eyes.

Avoid a perfectly straight square hairline.

## 5.3 CROWN

Build the crown from deliberately arranged voxel masses.

Include:

- Central hair volume.
- Layered top blocks.
- Slightly raised sections.
- Dark brown shadows between layers.
- Subtle variations in height and depth.

The crown should look sculpted, not randomly assembled.

## 5.4 HIGH PONYTAIL / BUN

This is a mandatory feature.

Model a compact high tied-up hair cluster behind or above the main head silhouette.

The tied-up volume should:

- Be visible in three-quarter and side views.
- Have a distinct shape from the main crown.
- Include a believable geometric attachment.
- Remain stable during head rotation.
- Not float above the head.
- Not intersect the back of the skull.

The reference uses a compact stylized tied hairstyle, not a long realistic ponytail.

Follow the supplied shape rather than inventing a new hairstyle.

## 5.5 HAIR ACCESSORY

Include the small yellow/gold hair clip or hair fastener visible in the reference.

Requirements:

- Genuine 3D geometry.
- Appropriate placement.
- Clearly recognizable gold/yellow accent.
- Stable head attachment.
- No Z-fighting.
- No oversized decorative accessory.

The hair accessory must remain visible from appropriate side angles.

## 5.6 SIDE VIEW

From the side:

- The fringe has visible depth.
- The crown has stepped layers.
- The tied-up section is recognizable.
- The ear remains properly visible.
- Hair does not intersect the ear.

## 5.7 BACK VIEW

The back is just as important as the front.

Create:

- Deliberately modeled rear hair volume.
- The visible tied-up section.
- Layered brown geometry.
- A controlled hair-to-neck transition.

